# ==========================================================================
#  Day 20 -- Agent SDK + MCP + subagents      .env.example
# ==========================================================================
#  COPY THIS FILE TO .env AND FILL IN THE FOUR VALUES.
#
#      cp .env.example .env          (PowerShell: copy .env.example .env)
#
#  This file is the TEMPLATE and is safe to commit: it has key names and no
#  values. The .env beside it holds the real ones and must never be committed
#  -- three of the four values below are credentials, and one of them does not
#  look like a credential until you read the note above it.
#
#  Where it goes: python-dotenv walks UP from the process's directory, so
#  either of these works --
#      week4_agentic_loop/Week4_agent_sdk/d22/agent_sdk/.env   (this folder)
#      week4_agentic_loop/.env                                 (shared by d20,
#                                                               d21 and d22)
#  One copy, not two. Two copies drift, and the nearer one wins silently.
#
#  Read by:  order_team_streamlit.py  (the Slack lines: that file only)
#            order_team_teaching.py  (everything above them)
#  The Streamlit app's sidebar shows a live check of all five lines, so start
#  it and look there rather than guessing which one is wrong.
# ==========================================================================


# --- 1. The model ---------------------------------------------------------
# Your Anthropic API key, from console.anthropic.com. The Agent SDK runs on
# top of the Claude Code CLI, so if you are already signed in to the CLI
# (`claude` -> /login) the SDK will use that session and this line can stay
# empty. Set it when you want the run billed to a specific API key, or when
# nothing is signed in -- CI, a container, someone else's machine.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...


# --- 2. The spreadsheet ---------------------------------------------------
# The long id out of the sheet's own URL:
#     https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/THIS_PART_HERE/edit
#
# Not a credential, but not public either: it is pasted verbatim into every
# agent's prompt, which is exactly why it lives here and not in the .py --
# the sheet id is CONFIGURATION, and configuration that is typed into code
# gets copied into a screenshot eventually.
#
# The sheet needs three tabs, spelled exactly:
#     Order    Order ID | Status | Carrier | ETA Days | Category | Date |
#              Customer Email | Customer Name
#     Delays   Order ID | Reason
#     Tickets  Ticket ID | Order ID | Issue | Resolution | Action By
#              (header row only -- the agent fills the rest; the first run
#               should create TKT-1)
GOOGLE_SHEET_ID=


# --- 3. The Google service account ----------------------------------------
# Absolute path to the service-account JSON you downloaded from Google Cloud.
# Forward slashes on Windows too.
#
# This path is handed to the MCP SERVER subprocess as an env var. No agent
# ever sees the key, so no prompt can leak it -- that is the whole reason the
# credential goes to the server and not into a tool argument.
#
# TWO THINGS GO WRONG HERE, both silently:
#   1. The sheet is not shared with the service account's email at all.
#      Open the JSON, copy "client_email", share the sheet with it.
#   2. It is shared as VIEWER. The earlier days only read. THIS day writes
#      Tickets tab, and a Viewer account fails that call with a permissions
#      error the agent will faithfully report back as write_failed.
#      It needs EDITOR.
SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PATH=C:/path/to/sheets-service-account.json


# --- 4. The escalation handoff (OPTIONAL) ---------------------------------
# Where a case goes once it stops being the agent's problem. Python POSTs the
# ticket here when Action By comes back as "Human" -- and only then. A webhook
# that fires on every ticket is one nobody reads.
#
# THE UUID IN THE PATH *IS* THE CREDENTIAL. Anyone holding this URL can post
# into your workflow. That is why it is in .env next to the API key and not
# in a .py file, however harmless a URL looks in a diff.
#
# Leave it empty and everything still runs: the ticket is still written, and
# the app says "not configured" instead of pretending it notified someone.
# A silently dropped notification is a case no human is ever told about.
ESCALATION_WEBHOOK_URL=


# --- 5. Slack: telling the team, in their channel (OPTIONAL) --------------
# The second half of the same handoff. When Action By comes back as "Human",
# Python POSTs the webhook above AND posts a card to Slack. Same condition,
# two destinations, neither replacing the other: the webhook feeds a workflow,
# the channel reaches the people who actually work the queue.
#
# Create a Slack app at api.slack.com/apps -> OAuth & Permissions:
#   1. add the BOT scope  chat:write
#      (add chat:write.public too if you want it to post to a channel it has
#       not been invited to -- otherwise invite the bot: /invite @your-app)
#   2. install to the workspace and copy the Bot User OAuth Token
#
# THE TOKEN IS THE CREDENTIAL. An xoxb- token posts as your bot to anything it
# can reach, until you revoke it. It belongs here, never in a .py.
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-...

# Where the escalation lands. Use the channel ID, not the name: names get
# renamed and the post then fails with channel_not_found. In Slack, open the
# channel -> View channel details -> the ID is at the bottom (C0123ABCDEF).
# A DM works too (D...), and so does a private channel the bot is a member of.
SLACK_CHANNEL_ID=C0123456789

# OPTIONAL. Only used to build links back into the workspace; leave it empty
# and everything still posts. Find it in any Slack URL: app.slack.com/client/
# T0123ABCDEF/...
SLACK_TEAM_ID=

# WHY THIS ONE IS CHECKED DIFFERENTLY FROM THE WEBHOOK:
# Slack answers HTTP 200 even when it refuses to post. A bad token, a channel
# the bot was never invited to, a missing scope -- all of them come back 200
# with {"ok": false, "error": "..."} in the body. Code that checks the status
# code alone reports a delivered handoff every single time and delivers
# nothing, which is worse than having no handoff, because now nobody is
# watching for it either. post_slack() reads `ok` out of the body and puts
# `error` on screen verbatim. The four you will actually hit:
#     invalid_auth       the token is wrong or revoked
#     channel_not_found  you used the name, or the wrong workspace
#     not_in_channel     invite the bot, or add chat:write.public
#     missing_scope      the token lacks chat:write
